          "text": "The user is asking me to explain the essence of \"Stage 12. Голое версионирование\" (Bare versioning) — what it is and why it's needed. They explicitly say \"пока что не давай мне шагов для выполнения, а объясни суть этого этапа\" — don't give me execution steps yet, explain the essence.\n\nThis is a teaching/explanation request. I'm in plan mode, read-only. I should just explain the concept, no file changes needed.\n\nLet me recall from AGENTS.md § 4.8 (лестница):\n\n| Версионирование | `kVersion` + `--version` + `CHANGELOG.md` | CMake `project(VERSION)` + git describe | появилась CMake-сборка |\n\nAnd § 5, layer 12: \"Голое версионирование. `kVersion` + `--version` + `CHANGELOG.md` (Keep a Changelog).\"\n\nAlso § 6.1 task context mentions versioning.\n\nSo I need to explain what \"bare versioning\" means: a constant `kVersion`, a `--version` command-line flag, and a CHANGELOG.md following \"Keep a Changelog\" format.\n\nThe user's background: C#, no native C++, learning. I should explain in Russian, with analogies, why versioning matters, what \"bare\" (голое) means vs \"industrial\" (промышленная).\n\nLet me explain:\n1. What versioning is — the version number attached to a program.\n2. Why it's needed — when you give someone (or your future self) a binary, or when you file a bug, you need to know which version it is.\n3. What \"голое\" (bare) means — simplest form: a hardcoded constant + a --version flag that prints it + a CHANGELOG.md.\n4. The analogy to C# — you've seen AssemblyVersion / version in .csproj, package versions, etc.\n5. What the industrial version is — CMake project(VERSION), git describe (auto version from git tags), etc.\n6. The three components: kVersion constant, --version flag, CHANGELOG.md.\n\nI should be concise but this is a teaching explanation, so a bit more detail is warranted. The AGENTS.md says be critical, mark [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ/ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ] and confidence. Actually the anti-flattery block is supposed to be pasted by the user as the second message — they said \"копирую его следующей репликой\". So I don't have it yet. But I can still apply a critical mindset.\n\nI should probably note that this is explanation, not steps. Keep it focused.\n\nLet me also consider: the user is on stage 12, which comes after layers 1-11. They've been through hello world, I/O, warnings, testing, cdb, separate build stages, CLI UX/help, bare logging. Now versioning.\n\nLet me structure my answer:\n\n1. Суть: что такое версия программы и зачем.\n2. Три компонента голого версионирования.\n3. Аналогия с C#.\n4. Почему \"голое\" и что потом \"промышленное\".\n\nI'll write in Russian, concise but educational. Since this is a plan-mode teaching question, I'll give the explanation directly.\n\nLet me not overdo length. But this is a \"explain the essence\" request, so a medium-length answer is appropriate. AGENTS says \"explain layers, don't skip\". I'll aim for clarity with analogy.\n\nI should mark confidence categories where relevant, per the spirit of § 4.2 (though the block hasn't been pasted yet, I can still be careful about facts).\n\nActually, let me think about what's genuinely important here for this student:\n\nThe core idea: a program artifact (the .exe you ship) needs an identity — a version number — so that:\n- When a bug is reported, you know which build it came from.\n- You can track what changed between builds.\n- You can tell if the artifact is stale.\n\n\"Голое\" versioning = the minimal version of this, done by hand:\n- `kVersion` — a named constant (not a magic string scattered around) that holds e.g. \"1.0.0\".\n- `--version` — the program, when run with this flag, prints the version and exits cleanly (exit code 0). This is part of the CLI UX layer (layer 10) — it's how a script or human can ask \"who are you?\"\n- `CHANGELOG.md` — a file in Keep a Changelog format (https://keepachangelog.com) that records, for each version, what was Added/Changed/Fixed/Removed. It's the human-facing history, tied to version numbers.\n\nWhy not rely on git log? Git log is developer-facing and noisy (every commit). CHANGELOG is user-facing: only notable changes, grouped by version, in a consistent format.\n\nThe versioning ties together: the constant in code ↔ the flag output ↔ the CHANGELOG entry. If you bump kVersion to 1.1.0, you must also add a \"1.1.0\" section to CHANGELOG. This discipline is the point.\n\nAnalogy to C#: AssemblyInfo.cs / `[assembly: AssemblyVersion(\"1.0.0.0\")]`, `<Version>` in .csproj, NuGet package versions. You've seen version numbers before; here you're doing it manually and tying it to a changelog.\n\nIndustrial later: CMake `project(VERSION 1.0.0)` gives a single source of truth, and `git describe` derives a version from git tags automatically (e.g., v1.0.0-3-gabc1234). That removes the \"human must keep kVersion and git tag in sync\" burden.\n\nI think I should present this and then ask if they want me to elaborate or confirm understanding, maybe offer a micro-thinking question. Actually they asked to just explain essence. I'll explain, and perhaps end with one reflective question to check understanding (per anti-flattery / active recall spirit) but not overwhelm.\n\nLet me write it.",
