ion must be consistent across places and someone maintains it.\n\nBut here's a critical point I should raise: the mentor said \"промышленная форма\" but the industrial form in § 4.8 is CMake `project(VERSION)` + git describe. The mentor ALSO added `--version` at each program and CHANGELOG.md. So actually the \"industrial\" version isn't fully automated — git describe isn't even mentioned by the mentor explicitly; the mentor listed: project(VERSION), --version per program, CHANGELOG.md.\n\nWait let me re-read the mentor's message: \"версия в `project(VERSION)`, `--version` у каждой программы, `CHANGELOG.md`. Для компонентов разберись, что именно фиксирует `builtin-baseline` в `vcpkg.json`, и доведи фиксацию до конца.\"\n\nSo the mentor's \"industrial form\" is actually:\n1. project(VERSION) in CMake\n2. --version at each program\n3. CHANGELOG.md\n4. Understand and finalize builtin-baseline in vcpkg.json\n\nThis is actually a hybrid — it's not the full \"git describe\" automation. It's project(VERSION) as single source of truth + per-program --version + CHANGELOG.\n\nNow, the student's question: should she do a small bare version first for understanding?\n\nMy role per AGENTS.md: be critical, don't just agree. The mentor is authoritative. But I should think about whether the bare version adds real value here or is just cargo-cult ritual compliance.\n\nLet me think about the \"не пропускаем слои\" principle. Its rationale is: \"перепрыгивание создаёт карго-культы (CMake поверх непонятого cl /c и link).\" The reason for not skipping is that the upper layer hides the lower layer's mechanism, so if you don't understand the lower layer, you cargo-cult the upper one.\n\nBut does this apply to versioning? The versioning bare layer's mechanism is: a constant in code, tied to a flag, tied to a changelog. The industrial layer (project(VERSION)) doesn't HIDE that mechanism — it's a different mechanism entirely (single source of truth in build config vs hand-maintained constant).