t's project(VERSION) + --version + CHANGELOG, no git describe automation yet. That's actually already a reasonable \"middle\" that gives her the bare-form concept (changelog + flag + single constant) while using CMake as source of truth.\n\nWait, but the mentor's directive might conflict with AGENTS.md's \"не пропускаем слои\". But actually the mentor IS the one who wrote AGENTS.md's philosophy. The mentor is explicitly authorizing the skip. So the mentor has decided the bare form isn't necessary here. My job is not to second-guess the mentor's authority but to help her understand WHY, and to help her catch what she might still be missing.\n\nBut — I should be critical per § 4.2. Let me think: is there a risk in skipping the bare form?\n\nRisk: She might not internalize that \"version is a human-maintained agreement across three places\" because CMake does it for her. But actually, in the mentor's plan, CMake does NOT maintain the changelog — SHE still maintains CHANGELOG.md by hand, and she still has to bump the version in project(VERSION) and add a changelog entry. So the \"agreement across places\" discipline is STILL partly manual: project(VERSION) ↔ CHANGELOG.md. That's exactly the bare-form lesson, just with the constant relocated from code to CMakeLists.\n\nSo actually the bare form and the mentor's plan both retain the core discipline. The only thing that changes is WHERE the version constant lives (code file vs CMakeLists). \n\nHmm, but there's one subtle thing the bare form teaches that the industrial form might obscure: in the bare form, you write `std::println(\"{}\", kVersion)` directly — you SEE the string in code. In industrial form, the version arrives via `-D` or a generated header, so it's slightly \"magic\" (where does this macro come from?). That's a small conceptual gap but worth flagging — she should understand how project(VERSION) flows into the program (via configure_file or target_compile_definitions), not just accept \"it works\".\n\nLet me