mit of the vcpkg catalog, which pins the exact versions of all dependencies (gtest) across all machines. That's \"dependency versioning\" — reproducibility.\n\nThis is the deeper insight: versioning has two faces:\n- Your artifact's identity (project VERSION → --version → CHANGELOG)\n- Your dependency graph's identity (builtin-baseline → reproducible gtest version)\n\nBoth are about \"pin identity so you can reproduce/reason about a specific build.\"\n\nLet me now also reflect: is there a real risk in her NOT doing the bare form? I think the risk is minimal and specific:\n- She might not internalize that the version string is \"just a value that has to be consistent\", because CMake does the flow for her. But she's already demonstrated she understands --version vs --help, and she's been meticulous about consistency across the whole project (her journal shows deep diligence). \n\nActually — being critical per §4.2 — let me challenge my own recommendation. Is there a reason she SHOULD do a tiny bare version? The \"не пропускаем слои\" rule says don't skip. But the mentor explicitly overrode that for this layer. And the mentor's override is well-justified: versioning is conceptually trivial compared to make→cmake→vcpkg, and she's already past the \"what is a version\" question.\n\nHowever, I should NOT just rubber-stamp the mentor either. Let me identify what she'd genuinely lose:\n1. The physical feel of \"the version constant lives in MY code and I print it directly\" — minor, she'll get the equivalent when she sees the version flow from CMake.\n2. Nothing else material.\n\nActually, one more angle: there's a subtle difference between the bare and industrial that IS worth her attention even without doing the full bare exercise: In bare versioning, `--version` prints a string that's IN the source code, so the program \"knows\" its version. In industrial, the version is INJECTED at build time (via -D or generated header). This means: the SAME source tree can produce binaries with different versions (e.g., a release build tagged 1.2.0 vs a dev build tagged 1.2.0-rc1). That's actually the CORE VALUE of industrial versioning, and it's worth her understanding: version becomes a BUILD PROPERTY, not a CODE property. This is the insight the mentor's plan will surface naturally.\n\nSo my recommendation crystallizes:\n\n1. Don't build a parallel bare-versioning system. The mentor is right. Doing a separate bare version (even \"small\") would be exactly the kind of \"вторая параллельная система\" that AGENTS.md §4.9/§4.1 forbids (it'd be a versioning source that then has to be reconciled with project(VERSION), and then removed — pure waste).\n\n2. But DO make sure you extract the bare-form's lesson while doing the industrial form, specifically:\n   a. Understand the FLOW: project(VERSION 0.1.0) → how does each program's --version know it? (generate a header via configure_file, or target_compile_definitions with the version macro). Don't accept \"it just works\" — trace it.\n   b. Understand the AGREEMENT: project(VERSION) ↔ CHANGELOG.md must move together. When you bump one, bump the other. This is exactly the bare-form discipline, just relocated.\n   c. Understand the THREE version-ish numbers and which is which (program version vs vcpkg.json \"version\" vs builtin-baseline) — this connects directly to the mentor's builtin-baseline instruction.\n\n3. On the builtin-baseline part: she already pinned it and added a check (2026-09-29 journal). The mentor's \"доведи фиксацию до конца\" — I should ask what's unfinished, OR note that I can see it's already pinned + checked, so maybe the remaining piece is something specific. Actually I don't know what \"пункты 3 и 4 из сообщения 9\" are. I should ask her what those are, OR flag that I need to know what \"довести до конца\" refers to since builtin-baseline already looks pinned.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the question scope. She asked a focused question: \"стоит ли мне делать голую версию пер