          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user answered my questions, and now has follow-up questions:\n1. Confirmed: only 8 task programs, gen_cloud is intentional. She asks to confirm her understanding: \"именно это 'продукт' и мы заботимся именно о его версиях, верно понимаю или нет?\"\n2. No --help.\n3. 0.1.0 okay. But asks: \"в этом номере есть какой-то смысл или это просто стандартное начало?\" — is there meaning to 0.1.0 or just a standard start?\n4. Doesn't understand question 4: \"версия какого пакета? и почему она живет отдельно\" — what package, why does it live separately.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Confirm/refine her understanding of #1.\n2. Answer #3 about 0.1.0 meaning.\n3. Explain #4 — the vcpkg.json \"version\" field, what it means, why it's separate.\n\nI'm still in plan mode. I need to answer these questions clearly, then present a finalized plan. Let me answer the conceptual questions well since she's learning.\n\nLet me address each.\n\n**Question 1 — confirm her understanding of \"продукт\":**\nShe said: \"именно это 'продукт' и мы заботимся именно о его версиях\". \n\nI should refine slightly: it's about \"the thing a user/human runs and might need to identify\". The test utilities (run_case, check) are internal infrastructure — they're part of the build/test machinery, not shipped deliverables. When someone reports \"your program is broken\", they mean t1/t2/t3/t4, not run_case. So --version belongs to the user-facing programs. That's the core of it.\n\nActually let me be more precise and critical. The reason isn't just \"product vs internal\". It's about the *identity of the artifact that travels to another person*. run_case/check are consumed by the test suite in the same build; they don't travel independently, and their \"version\" is always implied by the source tree they came from. The 8 task programs are the deliverables that could be handed to someone else (or re-run later) where you need to know \"which build is this\".\n\nHer understanding is essentially right. I'll confirm and sharpen the reason: it's about *which artifacts have an identity that outlives the build context*.\n\n**Question 3 — meaning of 0.1.0:**\nThis is a good question. Is 0.1.0 \"standard start\" or meaningful?\n\n[ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ] SemVer: 0.x.y means \"initial development, API not stable yet\". Specifically:\n- 0.1.0 = first minor increment of a 0-major version = \"pre-1.0, not yet stable/production, API may change freely\".\n- 0.0.1 = very first patch.\n\nSo 0.1.0 carries a signal: \"this is not yet 1.0.0, i.e. not production-ready, breaking changes allowed without major bump.\" That's actually MEANINGFUL and appropriate for her project — it's a training project, not a shipped product. So 0.1.0 isn't arbitrary: the leading 0 declares \"pre-release/development\", and 1 in the minor position says \"second minor iteration\" (0.0.x would be the first).\n\nActually, let me be careful about accuracy. SemVer's spec:\n- Major 0 is \"initial development. Anything may change at any time. The public API should not be considered stable.\"\n- Version 0.1.0 means: major=0 (unstable), minor=1 (first minor release), patch=0.\n\nIs 0.1.0 the \"standard start\"? Common starting points: 0.1.0 (most common for a project that's been through several iterations but still pre-1.0) or 0.0.1 or 1.0.0 (if you consider it already \"done\" in some sense).\n\nGiven she's been working on this for months (tasks 1-4 done with full test chain), 0.1.0 is a reasonable \"it has real substance but is not a stable 1.0 product\". So there IS a meaning: the leading 0 = \"pre-1.0, unstable API\", and 0.1.0 = \"first meaningful increment, not the first-ever commit\".\n\nAlso note: it happens to match vcpkg.json's \"version\" which she set earlier to 0.1.0 — but that was probably set when setting up the manifest, possibly also \"just a placeholder\". So the coincidence is convenient.\n\nI should also mention: the actual VALUE of the number at this stage is less important than the *consistency* — what matters is that project(VERSION), CHANGELOG, and --version all agree, and that the number only changes in a disciplined way. The starting value is a declaration of intent (0 = unstable, not-yet-1.0).\n\n**Question 4 — vcpkg.json \"version\":**\nShe asks: \"версия какого пакета? и почему она живет отдельно\".\n\nI need to explain what vcpkg.json is and what its \"version\" field means.\n\nContext: vcpkg is a package manager. vcpkg.json is the *manifest* — it describes your project AS a package (in vcpkg's \"manifest mode\"). Fields:\n- \"name\": the name of the project/package.\n- \"version\": the version of THIS package (your project as seen by vcpkg).\n- \"builtin-baseline\": the catalog commit pinning dependency versions.\n- \"dependencies\": what libraries your project needs (gtest).\n\nSo the \"version\" in vcpkg.json is: \"if someone were to consume MY project as a vcpkg package, what version would it be\". It's the version of the project-as-a-package.\n\nNow — why does it live separately from project(VERSION)?\n\nTwo reasons:\n1. **Different consumers/contexts.** project(VERSION) is read by CMake (the build system) and exposed to your program via --version. vcpkg.json[\"version\"] is read by vcpkg (the package manager) when it installs dependencies / identifies the package. They're two different tools with two different notions of \"who am I\".\n2. **They're often the SAME number in practice** — many projects keep them in sync. But they're not *forced* to be the same by any tool. vcpkg doesn't read project(VERSION), and CMake doesn't read vcpkg.json[\"version\"]. So if you change one and forget the other, they silently diverge.\n\nSo the design question I was raising: is there a risk that you now have \"0.1.0\" in TWO files (vcpkg.json and, after this stage, CMakeLists.txt project(VERSION)) plus CHANGELOG, and they can drift? \n\nThe honest answer for her project: since she's not publishing her project as a vcpkg package, the vcpkg.json \"version\" field is essentially vestigial — vcpkg manifest-mode requires (or at least expects) a name and version, but nobody consumes her package. So the \"version\" there is mostly a placeholder that vcpkg needs for the manifest to be valid.\n\nActually, is \"version\" REQUIRED in vcpkg.json? Let me think. In vcpkg manifest mode, the manifest needs at minimum \"name\". \"version\" — I believe for a \"consumer\" manifest (a project that consumes deps, not publishes), \"version\" is optional but recommended. Actually, I'm not 100% sure. Let me mark this as [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] and suggest checking vcpkg docs, OR I could actually check.\n\nActually, I recall: vcpkg manifest `\"version\"` is required if you want to use versioning/registries, but for a simple consumer manifest it might be optional. Hmm. But her vcpkg.json HAS \"version\": \"0.1.0\" already. Let me not over-claim. I'll say: the \"version\" field describes your project as a package; since you don't publish, it's a placeholder, but vcpkg's manifest format includes it as the package's own version. I'll flag the \"is it required?\" question as something to verify in vcpkg docs if she cares, but it doesn't block this stage.\n\nLet me be accurate and honest. I could verify by checking vcpkg docs via webfetch, but I'm in plan mode and it's a minor point. Actually, being a good critical assistant, I could quickly check. But it might not be worth a webfetch. Let me mark the uncertainty clearly instead.\n\nActually, let me think about whether this matters for the plan. The key point for her: there are now (potentially) two files carrying \"0.1.0\", and the discipline is to not let them silently drift. The clean resolution:\n- Declare project(VERSION) as the authoritative program version (mentor said so).\n- vcpkg.json[\"version\"] is a separate field (package version) that vcpkg wants; since she doesn't publish, it's fine to leave as-is or even note it's unused. The main thing is to KNOW they're separate and not treat them as one.\n\nActually, there might be an even cleaner insight: some projects make CMake READ the version from vcpkg.json, or vice versa, to have ONE source. But that's over-engineering here and the mentor explicitly said \"версия в project(VERSION)\". So: project(VERSION) wins; vcpkg.json[\"version\"] is left alone / documented.\n\nLet me now also decide: do I need to ask any more questions, or can I present the final plan? \n\nHer message answered 1, 2, 3, 4 (with questions embedded). She asked me questions in 1, 3, 4. I should answer those, and for #4 once I explain, she may or may not want to keep vcpkg.json[\"version\"] synced. I think I can explain #4 and then propose a default (leave vcpkg.json version as-is, document the distinction in README, don't add auto-sync), and present the final plan.\n\nActually there's still a small open design point I raised in question 4 that she now needs to answer after understanding. But I can propose the default and let her confirm.\n\nAlso, I should now finalize the plan since the scope is settled (8 programs, no --help, 0.1.0). Let me present a clean final plan.\n\nLet me also reconsider one more design detail I flagged earlier: the --version handling duplication (8 programs). And the delivery mechanism (configure_file vs -D). And whether to generate version.h or version.hpp.\n\nLet me settle these in the plan with recommendations:\n\n1. **Delivery mechanism**: configure_file → generate `version.h`. But wait — I should reconsider .h vs .hpp and the macro vs constexpr tension.\n\nGiven both C and C++ programs exist, and the project distinguishes .h (C, #ifndef guard) vs .hpp (C++, #pragma once):\n- Simplest cross-language: one generated `version.h` with #ifndef guard + `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.1.0\"`. Both C and C++ include it.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the project's rule says .h = C with #ifndef guards. A generated version.h fits that. C++ including a .h with a macro is fine.\n\nAlternatively, generate two: `version.h` (for C, macro) and `version.hpp` (for C++, `inline constexpr std::string_view`). More idiomatic per-language, but more machinery.\n\nFor the learning value and simplicity, I'll recommend the single `version.h` with a macro, but note the option. Actually, let me think about what's more pedagogically clean and what she'd prefer. She's careful about idiomatic C++. A single macro header is the standard, low-friction answer and is what most projects do (config.h). I'll go with single version.h + macro, note the C++ constexpr alternative as an option she can choose.\n\nHmm, but actually — is there value in a version.hpp with constexpr for C++? The mentor's §4.3 is strong about idiomatic C++. But a version string macro is a widely accepted exception (it's build-generated, not business logic). I'll present single version.h as recommended, flag the constexpr alternative honestly.\n\n2. **--version handling**: 8 programs. Each needs, at the top of main (before normal validation):\n```cpp\n// C++\nif (argc >= 2 && std::string_view{argv[1]} == \"--version\") {\n    std::println(\"{}\", INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nFor C:\n```c\nif (argc >= 2 && strcmp(argv[1], \"--version\") == 0) {\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nDuplication: 4 C + 4 C++ = 8 near-identical blocks (2 distinct shapes). Per \"повторила дважды — выноси\", this is a candidate for a shared helper. But the helper would be tiny. \n\nOptions:\na) Shared helper in common/ (both C and C++ versions), e.g. `bool print_version_and_exit(int argc, char** argv)` returning whether it printed. 8 programs call it.\nb) Inline per program.\n\nGiven the project's common/ culture and that it's 8×, I lean toward a small shared helper. BUT — the helper needs to be in BOTH string_utils.c/.cpp (or a new common module). Adding a version module just for this is heavy. Where would it live? It depends on the version header + std::println/printf. Could go into string_utils (already shared) or a new tiny module.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: the version flag logic is trivial (compare one arg), but the \"print version\" part depends on the version header. A helper `handle_version_flag` would print and return bool.\n\nHmm, but actually is this worth a shared function? It's 4 lines, and each main is already somewhat self-contained (they each have their own error handling). The mentor said \"--version у каждой программы\" — implying each program gets it. A helper is DRY but adds indirection for tiny logic.\n\nLet me think about the \"повторила дважды — выноси\" rule honestly. It's 8 repetitions of essentially the same 4-line block. That's well past \"twice\". So by the project's own rule, it should be a shared function. I'll recommend a shared helper, and note the tradeoff (tiny function, but 8× repetition justifies it). Actually, I realize there's a cleaner framing: the C and C++ versions are *different code* (strcmp vs string_view), so it's really 4× repetition within each language. Still past \"twice\".\n\nI'll recommend: shared helper `handle_version_flag(argc, argv)` in common (both C and C++), returning true if it printed the version (caller does `return 0;`). This keeps the version header included in ONE place (common) rather than 8. That's actually a strong argument: the `#include \"version.h\"` then lives in common, not in 8 mains. Cleaner.\n\nWait, but then how do the 8 programs know the version? They don't need to include version.h at all — the helper in common includes version.h and prints. So the version header dependency is localized to common. That's clean.\n\nBut there's a wrinkle: common/ is built as static AND shared libs (string_utils_c_static, string_utils_cpp_static, common_c_static, common_c_shared, common_cpp_static, common_cpp_shared). Adding the version flag helper means it needs to be in the right module that all 8 programs link. t1 links common_c_static (geometry only? let me recall).\n\nFrom CMakeLists: \n- t1_cpp l