          "text": "The user is asking: why do we do the include guard `#ifndef IRINA_VERSION_H / #define IRINA_VERSION_H` at all, rather than... wait, let me parse her question carefully.\n\nShe wrote: \"а чего мы делаем #ifndef IRINA_VERSION_H #define IRINA_VERSION_H ... а не сразу #ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\" #define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"\"\n\nI think she's confusing two DIFFERENT macros:\n1. The include guard: `IRINA_VERSION_H` — its job is to prevent double-inclusion of the header.\n2. The version value macro: `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` — its job is to hold the version string.\n\nShe's asking: why have the include guard at all, instead of just directly guarding with the version macro itself?\n\nActually, re-reading her question: she's proposing `#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\" ...` — which is malformed. She might be confusing the include guard with the value macro. She seems to think the `#ifndef` is about the version, when actually the `#ifndef IRINA_VERSION_H` is the include guard (a separate thing), and `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"` is the actual version value.\n\nSo the core confusion: she doesn't see why there are TWO macros. Let me explain clearly.\n\nThe two things are different concerns:\n\n1. **Include guard** (`IRINA_VERSION_H`): prevents the header's *contents* from being processed twice if it's included twice in the same translation unit. This is a structural thing every header has. Its \"value\" doesn't matter — it's just a unique marker name.\n\n2. **The value macro** (`INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION`): carries the actual data (the version string \"0.1.0\") that the program uses.\n\nHer proposed alternative `#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION ... #define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"` is wrong because:\n- If you use `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` as the include guard, then the guard condition checks whether the VALUE macro is already defined — which would break things: the whole point of the value macro is that it gets DEFINED, and then if the header is included twice, `#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` would be false (it's already defined), so the header content would be skipped — but that's actually the same effect as a guard in this case... \n\nWait, let me think more carefully. Actually there's a subtle point. If the header ONLY contains `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"`, then using `#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION / #define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` as the guard WOULD technically \"work\" for preventing redefinition of the same macro. But it's conflating two roles, and it has a real problem: the guard macro and the value macro are the same symbol. If some OTHER file also wanted to check \"is the version available?\", the presence of the macro as a guard would be indistinguishable. Also, more importantly, it's fragile: if the value ever needs to be `#undef`'d or redefined, or if the header grows to contain more than one definition, the single-macro approach breaks.\n\nBut the cleaner, more important reason: the include guard's job is purely \"has this file been included already?\" and the value macro's job is \"what's the version?\". Mixing them means one symbol is doing two jobs, which is confusing and breaks the moment the header has more than one thing in it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what she might actually be confused about. She might be asking: \"why does the header need an include guard at all for such a simple one-line content? Since it's just a #define of the same thing, including it twice is harmless.\"\n\nThat's a reasonable question. The honest answer: \n- The include guard is standard hygiene for ALL headers, regardless of content.\n- For a header containing only `#define`, double inclusion would just re-define the same macro to the same value, which is actually legal in C/C++ (redefining a macro to the identical token sequence is allowed, no error). So strictly, for THIS specific trivial header, the guard is not strictly *required* to avoid errors.\n- BUT: it's a convention/protection for the future — the moment you add anything else (a function declaration, another macro, a constant), double inclusion breaks. And the project rule (§5 layer 2) mandates guards on .h files. Consistency matters.\n\nActually wait — is redefining a macro to identical text legal? Yes. C standard: a macro can be redefined if the redefinition is identical. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]. But `@PROJECT_VERSION@` is replaced at configure time, so after generation, the header has `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.1.0\"` — if included twice, it's `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.1.0\"` twice = identical, so no error. So technically the guard is belt-and-suspenders here.\n\nBut the deeper answer to her confusion: the guard (`IRINA_VERSION_H`) and the value (`INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION`) are TWO different things, and she's trying to merge them. Let me explain the distinction clearly and simply.\n\nLet me also address her literal proposed syntax which is broken:\n`#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"` — this is not valid: `#ifndef` takes just a macro NAME, not a name + value.\n\nSo the answer structure:\n1. These are TWO different macros with two different jobs.\n   - `IRINA_VERSION_H` = include guard: \"did this file get included already?\" Its value doesn't matter; it's just a unique flag name. Present in EVERY header.\n   - `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` = the actual data: \"what version string?\" This is what string_utils will print.\n2. Her alternative would merge them into one symbol, which:\n   - is syntactically wrong (`#ifndef NAME` takes only a name, no value),\n   - and even if written \"correctly\", conflates two roles: the flag \"file included?\" and the value \"0.1.0\" become the same symbol, which is confusing and breaks as soon as the header contains more than one thing.\n3. Why the guard at all for a one-line header: convention + project rule + future-proofing; for this specific trivial case redefining identical macro is technically legal, but consistency says every .h gets a guard.\n\nLet me also relate to something she already knows: she has seen `string_utils.h` with `#ifndef IRINA_STRING_UTILS_H / #define IRINA_STRING_UTILS_H` — same pattern. And her `.h` rule. So this is familiar territory; she just momentarily confused the guard with the value.\n\nLet me write a concise, clear answer. Use a small analogy maybe.\n\nAnalogy: The include guard is like a \"документ уже открыт?\" закладка/флаг. The value macro is the actual content (\"0.1.0\").\n\nLet me keep it tight — she asked \"а чего мы делаем X ... а не Y\", a focused conceptual question. Answer concisely but completely.\n\nI'll answer in Russian, concise.\n\nNo tools needed. Just answer.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n---\n\nЭто **два разных макроса с двумя разными задачами**. Ты их сейчас слила в один — отсюда путаница.\n\n`IRINA_VERSION_H` — это **include guard**, а не версия. Его единственная работа: «этот файл уже был включён?» Значение у него нет — это просто уникальный флажок. Он есть в каждом твоём `.h` (`IRINA_STRING_UTILS_H`, `IRINA_GEOMETRY_H`) и не имеет отношения к версии.\n\n`INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` — это **само значение**, строка `\"0.1.0\"`, которую потом напечатает `string_utils`.\n\nТвоя запись `#ifndef INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\"` невалидна: `#ifndef` принимает только *имя* макроса, без значения. Но даже если записать «правильно» (guard = value = один символ), это сливает две роли в одну: один и тот же символ одновременно означает «файл включён?» и «версия = 0.1.0». Как только в заголовке появится что-то ещё (константа, функция), это развалится, и читателю не ясно, за что отвечает символ.\n\nПочему guard вообще нужен, если в файле одна строка `#define`: формально повторное включение `#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.1.0\"` дважды — легально (переопределение макроса на идентичный текст не ошибка) [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]. Но guard — это конвенция для всех `.h` (твоё прави