print the literal `%s` (well, actually `{}` is the format spec; `%s` would just be literal text `%s`, and the argument `INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION` would be... actually `std::println` with extra args beyond the format string is an error, or the format string `%s` has no `{}` placeholder so the arg is unused → compile error or warning).\n\nActually, `std::println(\"%s\", INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION)` — the format string is `\"%s\"`, which contains no `{}` placeholders. Passing an extra argument when the format string has no placeholders is a compile-time error in std::format (it's checked at compile time). So this would be a compile error. OR, more precisely, `std::format` requires that the number of arguments matches the placeholders. `\"%s\"` has zero placeholders, but one arg is passed → compile error.\n\nSo the C++ version is broken in multiple ways:\n1. `std::println(\"%s\", ...)` — wrong format syntax (should be `std::println(\"{}\", ...)` or `std::println(INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION)`).\n2. `argv[1] == \"--version\"` — pointer comparison, should be string_view comparison.\n\nThe C version:\n1. `argv[1] == \"--version\"` — pointer comparison, should be `strcmp`.\n2. `bool` without `<stdbool.h>`.\n\nAlso, `printf(\"%s\", INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION)` in C is correct (printf uses %s). But `argv[1] == \"--version\"` is wrong.\n\nAlso — there's another issue: the include `#include \"../generated/version.h\"` — relative path `../generated/version.h`. Let me think about whether this resolves correctly. string_utils.c is in `common/`. The generated version.h is in `build/debug/generated/version.h`. So `../generated/version.h` from `common/string_utils.c` would be `common/../generated/version.h` = `<repo>/generated/version.h` — but the generated file is in `build/debug/generated/`, NOT `<repo>/generated/`. So `../generated/version.h` as a relative include would NOT find it!\n\nWait — but I told her to add `include_directories(${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated)` and use `#include \"version.h\"`. She instead wrote `#include \"../generated/version.h\"`. \n\nHmm, let me think. The include search: `#include \"version.h\"` searches first relative to the including file's directory (common/), then the include dirs. `#include \"../generated/version.h\"` searches relative to common/ → `<repo root>/generated/version.h`.\n\nBut the generated file is at `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated/version.h` = `build/debug/generated/version.h` (absolute: `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\build\\debug\\generated\\version.h`).\n\nSo `../generated/version.h` from common/ would be `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\generated\\version.h` — which does NOT exist. The file is at `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\build\\debug\\generated\\version.h`.\n\nSo the `../generated/` path is wrong UNLESS she put the generated file somewhere else. But wait — she said she ran `cmake --preset debug` and \"вижу version.h с 0.1.0 внутри\" (step 2 verification). So the generated file IS at build/debug/generated/version.h (as I specified in the plan).\n\nBut then `#include \"../generated/version.h\"` in string_utils.c (in common/) would resolve to `inobitec_stud/generated/version.h`, which doesn't exist.\n\nHold on — let me reconsider. Did she actually wire `include_directories`? She asked in this message \"include_directories в самом начале верно?\" suggesting she did put it. But she ALSO wrote `#include \"../generated/version.h\"` instead of `#include \"version.h\"`.\n\nSo there are potentially TWO mechanisms fighting:\n1. `include_directories(${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated)` → enables `#include \"version.h\"`.\n2. She wrote `#include \"../generated/version.h\"` which is a relative path that won't resolve to build/debug/generated.\n\nSo the relative include `../generated/version.h` is likely WRONG and will fail to compile.\n\nActually wait — I need to reconsider. Maybe she didn't add include_directories and instead is relying on the relative `../generated/` path? But `../generated/` from common/ points to repo-root/generated, not bui