          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Three questions:\n\n1. Explain why `@PROJECT_VERSION@` in the template but `project(... VERSION 0.1.0 ...)` — why in one place it's \"version\" and in the other \"PROJECT_VERSION\"?\n\n2. What else to add to changelog — list, not ready text.\n\n3. The output shows all 8 programs print 0.1.0. Does it all work?\n\nLet me answer each.\n\n**Question 1: @PROJECT_VERSION@ vs project(VERSION ...)**\n\nThe `VERSION` in `project(... VERSION 0.1.0)` is an ARGUMENT to the `project()` command — it's the keyword that tells CMake \"here's the version number\". It's not a variable; it's a command argument.\n\n`PROJECT_VERSION` is the VARIABLE that CMake sets as a RESULT of calling `project(... VERSION ...)`. When you write `project(name VERSION 0.1.0)`, CMake sets (among others) the variable `PROJECT_VERSION` to `\"0.1.0\"`.\n\nSo `@PROJECT_VERSION@` in the template refers to the variable `PROJECT_VERSION`, which was populated by the `VERSION` argument to `project()`.\n\nThe `@VAR@` syntax in configure_file: `@` delimiters enclose a VARIABLE name. `configure_file` substitutes `@PROJECT_VERSION@` with the VALUE of the CMake variable `PROJECT_VERSION`.\n\nSo: `VERSION` = the argument name in `project()`. `PROJECT_VERSION` = the variable CMake creates. `@PROJECT_VERSION@` = \"substitute the value of the variable PROJECT_VERSION here\".\n\nAlso there's `PROJECT_VERSION_MAJOR`, `PROJECT_VERSION_MINOR`, `PROJECT_VERSION_PATCH` — CMake also sets these. And `CMAKE_PROJECT_VERSION` for the top-level project (vs `PROJECT_VERSION` which is the most recent `project()` call's version). Since there's only one project(), PROJECT_VERSION == CMAKE_PROJECT_VERSION.\n\nLet me explain with an analogy: `project(... VERSION 0.1.0)` is like a function call `project(name, VERSION=0.1.0)` — VERSION is the parameter name. The function then sets the variable `PROJECT_VERSION = 0.1.0`. The template reads the variable.\n\nActually, a cleaner framing: `VERSION` is the keyword/flag you pass; `PROJECT_VERSION` is the variable name that CMake later exposes. They're different names because they serve different roles (input keyword vs output variable).\n\nLet me be precise and correct. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ].\n\nActually, let me double-check the exact relationship. From CMake docs: `project(<PROJECT-NAME> [VERSION <major>[.<minor>[.<patch>[.<tweak>]]]] ...)`. When VERSION is given, CMake sets variables:\n- `PROJECT_VERSION`\n- `PROJECT_VERSION_MAJOR`, `PROJECT_VERSION_MINOR`, `PROJECT_VERSION_PATCH`, `PROJECT_VERSION_TWEAK`\n- `PROJECT_VERSION_STRING` (I think this is for `VERSION` command / package, not project... actually `PROJECT_VERSION` is the full string).\n\nAlso `CMAKE_PROJECT_VERSION` and related for the top-level project.\n\nSo `PROJECT_VERSION` = \"0.1.0\" as a string.\n\nThe `@ONLY` in configure_file means only `@VAR@` patterns are replaced. `@PROJECT_VERSION@` → the value.\n\nSo my answer: `VERSION` is the argument name; `PROJECT_VERSION` is the variable CMake fills. `@PROJECT_VERSION@` asks configure_file to splice the variable's value.\n\n**Question 2: what else to add to changelog (list, not text)**\n\nKeep a Changelog categories: Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, Security.\n\nFor her project's 0.1.0 initial release, sensible things to consider listing:\n- Added: the 4 tasks (each in C and C++), the common module (geometry, string_utils, parse_codes), the build system (CMake + presets), vcpkg + gtest, test infrastructure (run_case, check, unit tests, acceptance tests), versioning (--version, CHANGELOG, project(VERSION)), builtin-baseline reachability check, formatting (clang-format via format-check).\n- Changed: nothing (it's 0.1.0, initial).\n- Fixed: nothing (initial release — no prior released version to fix).\n- The \"known limitations\" — should these go in CHANGELOG? No, known limitations belong in README (already there). CHANGELOG is about what changed per version, not persistent limitations.\n\nSo I should suggest: list the logical groups of what was added, and note that \"Changed/Removed/Fixed/Security\" sections are empty for an initial release (and that's fine — don't pad them).\n\nAlso worth noting: the date in the `## [0.1.0] - YYYY-MM-DD` header.\n\nAlso: should the changelog mention the encoding? No.\n\nAlso: maybe a note about the reference implementation vs generator decision? Not really changelog material — that's design history, not \"what changed in this version\".\n\nLet me also mention: the changelog should be at a level that a READER (e.g., mentor) understands what this version delivers. So: build system, dependencies pinning, the 4 programs, the test suite, the versioning itself.\n\nLet me enumerate concretely as a list of TOPICS (not text):\n1. Четыре задачи (1–4), каждая в двух реализациях C17 и C++23 — можно перечислить по именам.\n2. Общий модуль (geometry + string_utils + parse_codes), статическая и разделяемая сборка, экспорт через dll.\n3. Система сборки CMake + пресеты Debug/Release.\n4. Зависимости: vcpkg (manifest), закреплённый builtin-baseline + проверка reachability, GoogleTest.\n5. Тестовая инфраструктура: юнит-тесты (exe return 0/1 и gtest smoke), acceptance-тесты (run_case, check), проверка stdout/stderr/exit-кода.\n6. Форматирование clang-format (format/format-check), включая проверку в ctest.\n7. Само версионирование: project(VERSION), --version, CHANGELOG.md.\n8. Документация README (структура, как собрать, известные ограничения, минимальные версии инструментов).\n\nAnd note: Changed/Removed/Fixed/Security — пусто для первого релиза, не заполнять искусственно.\n\n**Question 3: does it all work?**\n\nThe output shows all 8 print `0.1.0`. So `--version` works for all 8. Exit code presumably 0 (she'd need to verify `%errorlevel%` but the output looks correct).\n\nBut I should be critical: this confirms `--version` prints the version for all 8. What's NOT yet confirmed:\n- Exit code = 0 (she didn't show it; `echo %errorlevel%` after each).\n- No regression in t