atches divergence. \n\nNow, is there a simpler way to express \"consistency\" without the generated file? E.g., use check --literal. Let me reconsider whether --literal is actually cleaner:\n\ncheck --equal-literal \"${PROJECT_VERSION}\" ${STDOUT_FILE}\n\nThis requires:\n1. Extend check.cpp to accept a literal string mode.\n2. Modify add_case to support literal mode (or write manual tests).\n\nvs Option A:\n1. file(WRITE) the version.\n2. Modify add_case for absolute expected path.\n\nBoth require modifying add_case. Option A also modifies check? No, Option A does NOT modify check (check still compares two files). Option A only: (a) file(WRITE), (b) add_case absolute-path handling.\n\nOption B: (a) check --literal, (b) add_case literal handling.\n\nOption A is arguably simpler (check unchanged, just generate a file). Also Option A reuses the exact normalization semantics of check (both files normalized identically). \n\nI'll recommend Option A.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider if modifying add_case is even needed. What if I don't use add_case for the version tests, but write them as a small dedicated block? The version tests are structurally simple (no real input, compare stdout to version). \n\nActually, add_case handles all the plumbing (run_case + check + fixtures). Reusing it is cleaner than writing manual add_test. The only gap is the absolute expected path. So a minimal add_case extension is justified.\n\nAlternatively — hmm, what if I generate version.expect directly into the SOURCE expect dir? No, that's a generated file in the repo (bad, violates \"эталоны генерятся на лету\" §4.9.3). Actually §4.9.3 says \"Эталоны генерятся на лету, в репо не хранятся\". So generating the version expect into the BUILD dir (not repo) is exactly right. Good, this aligns with the course policy.\n\nSo Option A is aligned: generate version.expect on-the-fly into build dir, not committed.\n\nLet me finalize the plan. Let me also decide on the input file. I'll check if t1_nu