tion, or just recommend.\n\nI'll present the plan with my recommendation (fold consistency into the 8 tests via ${PROJECT_VERSION}), explain WHY (no hardcoded \"0.1.0\" anywhere; auto-tracks version bumps; catches wiring divergence), and note that this satisfies both of the mentor's bullets simultaneously. I'll ask her to confirm the two key decisions:\n1. Use ${PROJECT_VERSION} (dynamic) — recommend yes.\n2. Fold consistency into 8 tests vs separate 9th test — recommend fold.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the mentor's wording once more: \"на каждую из 8 программ — --version → exit 0 + stdout содержит 0.1.0; консистенси-тест «вывод --version == PROJECT_VERSION из CMake»\".\n\nReading it as a list: two requirements. If I fold, then \"stdout содержит 0.1.0\" becomes \"stdout == PROJECT_VERSION (which is currently 0.1.0)\". And the \"консистенси-тест\" is inherently satisfied. So folding satisfies both.\n\nBut maybe the mentor wants them SEPARATE: the 8 tests verify behavior (that --version works at all), and ONE test verifies the version VALUE equals PROJECT_VERSION. If separate:\n- 8 tests: exit 0 + stdout CONTAINS \"0.1.0\" (or just exit 0 + non-empty stdout).\n- 1 test: --version == PROJECT_VERSION.\n\nBut separating them with a hardcoded \"0.1.0\" reintroduces the hardcode problem for the 8 tests. Unless the 8 tests use \"exit 0 + stdout non-empty\" (not checking the specific value), and the 1 consistency test checks the exact value.\n\nHmm, that's a cleaner separation actually:\n- 8 tests: --version → exit 0 + stdout is non-empty (or contains SOME version). This verifies each program HAS --version wired.\n- 1 consistency test: --version output == PROJECT_VERSION. This verifies the VALUE is correct.\n\nBut \"stdout non-empty\" is a weak assertion. And checking \"contains 0.1.0\" hardcodes.\n\nI think the cleanest is: all 8 tests assert exit 0 + stdout == ${PROJECT_VERSION}. This is both behavior (works) AND consistency (correct value). No hardcode, no separate test. This is what I'll recommend.\n\nActually, I realize there might be value in NOT over-thinking and just presenting the recommendation clearly with the reasoning, and letting the user decide. Let me write a focused plan.\n\nLet me structure the plan:\n\n1. Design decision: use ${PROJECT_VERSION} (not hardcode) → makes all 8 tests inherently consistency tests. Explain why.\n2. Mechanism: generate version.expect at configure time + extend add_case for absolute expected path.\n3. Exact changes (3 files):\n   - tests/CMakeLists.txt: modify add_case (absolute expected path), OR add file(WRITE).\n   - cases.cmake: file(WRITE) version.expect + 8 add_case lines.\n4. Input file: reuse existing empty file.\n5. Verification: ctest --preset full, count should go 521 → 537.\n\nLet me also double check: is there an issue with the `VERSION_EXPECT` path and how check normalizes? The check does file_to_bytes_string on the expected path, then rm_crlf_symbols. file(WRITE) writes \"0.1.0\" (no newline). So expected = \"0.1.0\". Good.\n\nWait, actually, one more: on Windows, does file(WRITE) write with \\r\\n or just the exact bytes? file(WRITE) writes the exact content string, which is \"0.1.0\" (no newline at all). So no \\r, no \\n. Good.\n\nLet me also consider: should I use EQ or CONTAINS for the version test? EQ is stricter and correct (stdout should be EXACTLY \"0.1.0\\n\" → \"0.1.0\"). CONTAINS would allow extra output. Since --version prints exactly the version and nothing else, EQ is correct and stricter. Use EQ. Good.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider: is there a concern that the version test needs to check stderr is empty? The mentor didn't ask. I'll note it as optional (course §4.9 policy 7 suggests checking all three channels, but the mentor's instruction is specific; I'll flag it as a decision).\n\nActually, thinking about it — for a thorough test per course policy, checking stderr is empty would be nice. But it's 8 more expect files (empty) or a check that stderr is empty