me also do the \"self-check\" — confidence assessment and \"find one more\".\n\nPotential additional issues to flag:\n1. `PROJECT_VERSION` vs `CMAKE_PROJECT_VERSION` — I'll suggest PROJECT_VERSION (matches version.h.in). Minor.\n2. The `file(WRITE)` location and whether it should be in tests/CMakeLists.txt before include(cases.cmake) vs inside cases.cmake. I'll put it in cases.cmake (it's test-data-ish).\n3. stderr-empty check — optional.\n4. Whether to also test that `--version` does NOT read stdin (i.e., works even with empty/blocked stdin) — actually this is implicitly tested if the input file is empty and the program returns 0 without hanging. Since run_case opens the input file (not a pipe that blocks), there's no hang risk. Not an issue.\n\nActually, one REAL potential gotcha I should double-check: does `add_case`'s `args` parameter correctly handle `\"--version\"` being passed? Looking at line 75: `${args}` is unquoted in add_test. If args is the CMake variable `\"--version\"` (with quotes), then `${args}` expands to `--version`. Then in add_test COMMAND, it becomes `--version` as a separate argument. run_case gets it at argv[7]. Then quote_arg wraps it. Good.\n\nBut wait — how exactly should I pass the arg? In cases.cmake, I'd write `add_case(t1_c_version t1_c \"--version\" t1_nul ...)`. The `\"--version\"` is a literal string argument to add_case. add_case's signature `function(add_case test_name exe args input ...)`. So args = \"--version\" (the string). Then `${args}` = `--version`. Good.\n\nActually, to be safe and match the existing style (which uses ${VERSION_ARG} or ${RAD1} variables), I'll define `set(VERSION_ARG \"--version\")` and pass `${VERSION_ARG}`.\n\nOK, I'm confident in the plan. Let me write it up.\n\nLet me also note the expected test count change: 521 → 537 (16 new tests: 8 code + 8 stdout).\n\nLet me write concisely.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider one more time whether modifying add_case is the best, or if there's something simpler I'm missing. \n\nAlternative: Instead of generating version.expect and modifying add_case, could I just... create a static committed expect file `tests/expect/version` = \"0.1.0\"? Then 8 tests use it with EQ. This is the SIMPLEST (no add_case change, no file(WRITE)). But it hardcodes \"0.1.0\" in a 9th place and does NOT satisfy \"== PROJECT_VERSION\" (the consistency requirement). So it fails the mentor's explicit requirement.\n\nSo the dynamic approach is needed. And the dynamic approach requires either generating a file (Option A) or extending check (Option B). Option A with add_case absolute-path support is clean.\n\nActually, wait — is there a way to avoid modifying add_case by having check read the expected from stdin or an env var? No, that's more invasive.\n\nOr: could I write the version.expect into the SOURCE dir's expect/ folder but gitignore it? That's hacky (generated file in source tree, gitignored). The build dir is cleaner.\n\nSo Option A (generate in build dir + add_case absolute path) is the way.\n\nLet me finalize. I'll write the plan.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the add_case modification location and whether it should also be reflected in a way that keeps existing behavior intact. The existing add_case calls pass expected_result as a bare name (e.g., t1_nul). The new IS_ABSOLUTE check: for \"t1_nul\" (not absolute), goes to else branch → source/expect/t1_nul (unchanged behavior). For \"${VERSION_EXPECT}\" (absolute), goes to if branch. So backward compatible. Good.\n\nNow write.\n\nLet me keep the plan focused and ask for confirmation on the one key decision (fold + dynamic), since I already have a strong recommendation.\n\nFinal structure:\n1. Confirm current understanding of infra (brief).\n2. Design decision (use ${PROJECT_VERSION}, fold consistency) + why.\n3. Concrete changes (3 files, snippets).\n4. Input file note.\n5. Verification (537 tests).\n6. One decision to confirm + self-check.\n\nI'll write it now.",
