m and concluded it was over-engineering).\n\nLet me verify the §4.9 policy 7 claim vs her actual practice. §4.9.7 says \"Тесты проверяют все три канала: stdout, stderr, exit-код\". But her actual implementation checks exit code + ONE stream per case. So there's a nuance: across the test SUITE, both stdout and stderr are covered (some tests check stdout, some check stderr), but not \"for each case, all three channels simultaneously\".\n\nActually, let me think about what §4.9.7 actually means. It says tests should check all three channels. Her implementation checks:\n- exit code: always (run_case).\n- stdout OR stderr: one of them, per case.\n\nSo for an ERROR case: exit code + stderr (checked), stdout (not checked — but should be empty).\nFor a SUCCESS case: exit code + stdout (checked), stderr (not checked — but should be empty).\n\nSo the \"third\" channel (stdout-on-error, or stderr-on-success) is NOT checked. This is a known, deliberate simplification.\n\nSo the honest answer: yes, her existing tests don't verify the \"unexpected\" stream is empty. This is a known limitation/deliberate design decision (documented in her journal discussion from 2026-09-04).\n\nSo my answer to her:\n1. I do NOT advise adding stderr-empty check now (I recommended \"не сейчас\").\n2. You're right — your existing tests don't check stderr-empty on success (or stdout-empty on error). Each case checks exit code + one stream. This was a deliberate decision from an earlier discussion (2026-09-04).\n\nLet me be concise and clear.\n\nActually, let me also double-check: is there a subtlety I'm missing about whether the check tool or run_case implicitly verifies the other stream? No — run_case just captures stdout and stderr to files; it only compares the exit code. check compares ONE file (the chosen stream) against expected. The other stream file is written but never inspected (unless a test explicitly checks it).\n\nSo the answer is clear.\n\nLet me also reflect on whether I should now rec