rcised and verified — stdout on success, stderr on error, exit code always. It's not saying \"each test asserts stdout AND stderr AND exit simultaneously\".\n\nBut the user's question is more subtle and pointed. She's asking: if we KNOW that in a given test the other stream should be empty, does §4.9.7 require checking it?\n\nLet me think about this critically and honestly, per the anti-flattery rules.\n\nThe honest answer has nuance:\n\n1. §4.9.7's literal text is about: (a) not swallowing stderr, (b) checking exact exit codes, (c) triggering error branches. The emphasis is on ERROR diagnostics being checked (the \"не глушить 2>$null\" is specifically about error messages being visible).\n\n2. The policy does NOT explicitly say \"assert the non-primary stream is empty.\" That's a stronger requirement than what's written.\n\n3. However, one could argue that \"все три канала\" implies completeness. But the concrete guidance in the parentheticals is specifically about error diagnostics, not about asserting emptiness of the silent stream.\n\nSo the honest answer: §4.9.7 does NOT strictly mandate \"assert the other stream is empty.\" Its actual mandates are:\n- exact exit code (always),\n- stdout content on success,\n- stderr content on error (not swallowed),\n- error branches triggered.\n\nChecking \"stderr empty on success\" / \"stdout empty on error\" is a REASONABLE EXTENSION but not literally required by the text.\n\nBut wait — I should be careful and critical. Let me think about whether there's a real bug risk in NOT checking the empty stream.\n\nThe risk of not checking \"stderr empty on success\": a program might print a stray diagnostic to stderr even on success, and no test would catch it. Is that a real concern? For these programs, on success they print to stdout and nothing to stderr. If a bug caused a stray stderr write on success, it'd go unnoticed. But this is a relatively minor risk.\n\nThe risk of not checking \"stdout empty on error\": a program might print partial output to stdout before erroring. This is actually a MORE real concern (e.g., the t4_c earlier bug where it printed passing points before hitting an error). Her journal 2026-08-27/28 documented this exact bug: \"t4 си печатает проходящую точку хотя возможно она ничего не должна печатать\". So there IS a real class of bug where stdout is non-empty on error.\n\nBut her test design currently doesn't check \"stdout empty on error\" either. This is the known limitation.\n\nSo the honest, critical answer:\n1. §4.9.7 as written mandates: exact exit codes, checking stderr diagnostics on errors (not swallowing), triggering error branches. It does NOT literally mandate \"assert the silent stream is empty.\"\n2. \"Assert the other stream is empty\" is a stronger, reasonable extension, but it's not what §4.9.7's text requires.\n3. Her current design (exit + one stream per case) actually DOES satisfy the core of §4.9.7: exact exit code always, stderr checked on errors (not swallowed), stdout checked on success, error branches triggered (she has io-fail, empty input tests, etc.).\n\nSo she's already compliant with §4.9.7 as written. The \"empty stream\" check is a potential FUTURE hardening, not a current requirement.\n\nI should also note: this is a genuinely interesting interpretation question, and I should give my honest read, mark it as [МНЕНИЕ] with appropriate confidence, and note that if she's unsure, she can ask the mentor (the ultimate authority on their own policy).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the exact wording once more: \"Тесты проверяют все три канала: stdout (рабочий вывод), stderr (диагностика ...), exit-код (...).\"\n\nThe phrase \"проверяют все три канала\" — \"tests check all three channels\" — could be read as \"across the test suite, all three channels are checked.\" That's the most natural reading: some tests check stdout, some check stderr, all check exit code. NOT \"each individual test checks all three.\"\n\nThe parenthetical about stderr is \"не глушить 2>$null, иначе штампы в сообщениях невидимы для тестов\" — this is about NOT redirecting stderr to null (which would hide error messages). It's about error diagnostics visibility, not about asserting stderr-empty-on-success.\n\nSo my reading: §4.9.7 does NOT mandate the empty-stream assertion. It mandates: exact exit code (always), stderr diagnostics checked on errors, stdout checked on success, error branches triggered.\n\nHer current design satisfies this. So she's fine.\n\nThe \"should I check empty stream\" question is a SEPARATE hardening, not required by §4.9.7.\n\nLet me also connect to the specific --version case: for --version, the \"stderr should be empty\" — is this worth checking? Given:\n- Her entire suite doesn't do empty-stream checks.\n- The mentor's spec only named exit + stdout.\n- §4.9.7 doesn't literally mandate it.\n\nSo: no, not required. Her current plan (exit + stdout for --version) is compliant.\n\nBut I should be honest and precise, and let her decide. I should present my read of §4.9.7 clearly and note it's [МНЕНИЕ], and that the mentor is the authority.\n\nLet me also think: is there a subtlety where \"проверяют все три канала\" could be interpreted strictly? Yes, a strict reading could say \"each test should verify all three.\" But that's not how her suite is built, and the parentheticals clarify the intent is about error diagnostics. I'll present the nuance honestly.\n\nLet me write a concise, clear answer.\n\nKey points:\n1. §4.9.7's concrete mandates: exact exit code always; stderr checked on errors (don't swallow 2>$null); stdout on success; error branches triggered.\n2. It does NOT literally say \"assert the silent stream is empty.\"\n3. \"Все три канала\" most naturally reads as \"across the suite, all three are exercised,\" not \"each case asserts all three.\"\n4. Her current design already satisfies §4.9.7 as written.\n5. The empty-stream check is a reasonable future hardening, but not mandated now, and doing it o