ctually happened with the negative control.\n\nWhen she hardcoded version.h.in to 0.9.9 (replacing @PROJECT_VERSION@ with 0.9.9):\n- version.h.in = \"0.9.9\" (literal)\n- Reconfigure: version.h → \"0.9.9\", version.expect → \"0.1.0\" (still from PROJECT_VERSION=0.1.0)\n- Rebuild: binary prints \"0.9.9\"\n- Test: binary \"0.9.9\" vs expect \"0.1.0\" → MISMATCH → stdout tests fail.\n\nHer explanation: \"код тестов так и остаётся нуль\" (the CODE tests still pass, exit code 0, correct — the binary still exits 0). \"а вот выход - сравнение не проходит\" (the stdout comparison fails, correct). \"т.к. по точке истины project - 0.1.0 сейчас. а в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение.\"\n\nWait, she says \"в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение\". This is slightly imprecise. Let me parse: \"по точке истины project - 0.1.0\" (project VERSION = 0.1.0) — correct. \"а в заголовке... другое значение\" — the HEADER (version.h, now 0.9.9) has a different value.\n\nBut the phrase \"по которому и делается эталон для сравнения\" (by which the expected value is made) — this is confusing. The EXPECTED value (version.expect) is made from PROJECT_VERSION (0.1.0), NOT from the header. The header (version.h → binary) is what prints 0.9.9. So:\n- The EXPECTED (version.expect) = 0.1.0 (from project VERSION).\n- The RESULT (binary) = 0.9.9 (from the hardcoded header).\n\nSo the mismatch is: result (0.9.9) != expected (0.1.0).\n\nHer sentence \"а в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение\" is WRONG — the эталон (expected) is NOT made from the header. The эталон is made from PROJECT_VERSION. The header produces the RESULT (binary output).\n\nSo the explanation has an error: she conflates \"заголовок\" (header, which produces the binary's result) with \"эталон\" (expected, which comes from PROJECT_VERSION). The header does NOT make the эталон; the header makes the RESULT.\n\nLet me re-read her exact words (lines 3902-3904):\n\"то есть - код тестов так и остаётся нуль, а вот выход - сравнение не проходит. т.к. по точке истины project - 0.1.0 сейчас. а в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение. то есть, вот такой тест ловит момент, когда началось отхождение от точки истины и произошло рассогласование\"\n\nSo she says: \"в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение\" = \"in the header, by which the expected-for-comparison is made, a different value\".\n\nThis is incorrect. The header (version.h) does NOT produce the эталон (expected). The эталон (version.expect) is produced by PROJECT_VERSION directly. The header produces the program's OUTPUT (the result).\n\nThe correct statement: \"в заголовке (version.h), из которого программа печатает свой вывод, — другое значение (0.9.9), а эталон (version.expect) сделан из project(VERSION)=0.1.0. Потому вывод (0.9.9) != эталон (0.1.0)\".\n\nSo there's a factual imprecision in the explanation: she says the header makes the эталон, but actually the header makes the result, and the эталон comes from project(VERSION).\n\nHmm, wait — let me be careful. Actually, could her wording be a slip? \"в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение\" — maybe she meant \"в заголовке... другое значение\" as a general statement, and \"по которому делается эталон\" is misattached. Regardless, the sentence as written is imprecise/wrong: the header doesn't make the эталон.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. There might be a deeper subtlety. Both version.h AND version.expect are generated from the SAME source of truth (PROJECT_VERSION) via DIFFERENT mechanisms:\n- version.h ← version.h.in (uses @PROJECT_VERSION@)\n- version.expect ← file(WRITE \"${PROJECT_VERSION}\")\n\nWhen she hardcodes version.h.in to 0.9.9, she breaks the FIRST mechanism (version.h no longer derives from PROJECT_VERSION), while version.expect STILL derives from PROJECT_VERSION. So the divergence is: version.h (and hence binary) = 0.9.9, but version.expect = 0.1.0.\n\nSo the \"заголовок\" (version.h) is what the BINARY uses to print, and it's now 0.9.9. The \"эталон\" (version.expect) is 0.1.0.\n\nHer sentence says the header \"делает эталон\" — wrong. The header makes the program's output.\n\nSo I should flag this as an imprecision/error in the explanation.\n\nActually, let me re-read once more very carefully, because it's a nuanced point:\n\n\"т.к. по точке истины project - 0.1.0 сейчас. а в заголовке, по которому и делается эталон для сравнения, другое значение.\"\n\nHmm, \"по которому и делается эталон\" — \"by which the эталон is made\". If she's saying the header makes the эталон, that's wrong. But maybe she's saying something garbled.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest reading is she made an error: she thinks the header produces the эталон, but actually the header produces the RESULT (program output), and the эталон comes from project(VERSION) directly.\n\nThis is a genuine error worth flagging, because it reveals a possible confusion about the data flow (which of the two generated artifacts produces \"result\" vs \"expected\").\n\nLet me make sure I have the data flow 100% right:\n1. project(VERSION 0.1.0) → PROJECT_VERSION = \"0.1.0\"\n2. version.h.in has \"@PROJECT_VERSION@\" → configure_file → version.h has \"#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.1.0\"\"\n3. string_utils.c/.cpp includes version.h, prints INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION → program stdout = \"0.1.0\"\n4. file(WRITE version.expect \"${PROJECT_VERSION}\") → version.expect = \"0.1.0\"\n5. Test: check compares stdout (from #3) vs version.expect (from #4).\n\nNegative control: hardcode version.h.in to \"#define INOBITEC_STUD_VERSION \"0.9.9\"\" (replace @PROJECT_VERSION@ with 0.9.9):\n- #2 → version.h = \"0.9.9\"\n- #3 → program stdout = \"0.9.9\"\n- #4 → version.expect = \"0.1.0\" (unchanged, still from PROJECT_VERSION)\n- #5 → \"0.9.9\" != \"0.1.0\" → stdout test fails.\n\nSo the header (versi